"A Cauchy-Davenport theorem for semigroups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.4203)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.1 years ago; Title: A Cauchy-Davenport theorem for semigroups\nAuthors: Salvatore Tringali\nTitle: A Cauchy-Davenport theorem for semigroups\nAuthors: Salvatore Tringali\nDate: Mon Oct 15 21:42:36 2012\n\nWe generalize the Davenport transform and use it to prove that, for a (possibly non-commutative) cancellative semigroup $\\mathbb A = (A, +)$ and non-empty subsets $X,Y$ of $A$ such that the subsemigroup generated by $Y$ is commutative, we have $|X + Y| \\ge \\min(\\omega(Y), |X| + |Y| - 1)$, where $\\omega(Y) := \\sup_{y_0 \\in Y \\cap \\mathbb A^{\\times}} \\inf_{y \\in Y \\setminus \\{y_0\\}} |<y - y_0>|$. This carries over the Cauchy-Davenport theorem to the broader setting of semigroups, and it implies, in particular, an extension of I. Chowla's and S.S. Pillai's theorems for cyclic groups and a notable strengthening of another generalization of the same Cauchy-Davenport theorem to commutative groups, where $\\omega(Y)$ in the above is replaced by the minimal order of the non-trivial subgroups of $\\mathbb A$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFor every DOSP \\(D\\), Early defines the *winding number* \\(w(D) \\in \\{0,1,\\ldots,n-1\\}\\), see Definition 4.2, and conjectured that the \\(h^*\\)-polynomial is given by (https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/319821CF8BA31B04F1C592E8985450AE/S2050509425101242a.pdf/equivariant-ehrhart-theory-of-hypersimplices.pdf)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last year; For every DOSP \\(D\\), Early defines the *winding number* \\(w(D) \\in \\{0,1,\\ldots,n-1\\}\\), see Definition 4.2, and conjectured that the \\(h^*\\)-polynomial is given by ... This conjecture was proved by Kim [14] and gives a satisfying answer to Stanley’s second problem. We now recount the story of Ehrhart theory for hypersimplices in the equivariant setting.\nFor every DOSP \\(D\\), Early defines the *winding number* \\(w(D) \\in \\{0,1,\\ldots,n-1\\}\\), see Definition 4.2, and conjectured that the \\(h^*\\)-polynomial is given by\n\n\\[\nh^*_{\\Delta_{k,n}}(t)=\\sum_D t^{w(D)}\n\\]\n\nwhere the sum is taken over all hypersimplicial \\((k,n)\\)-DOSPs. This conjecture was proved by Kim [14] and gives a satisfying answer to Stanley’s second problem. We now recount the story of Ehrhart theory for hypersimplices in the equivariant setting.\n\n**Equivariant Ehrhart theory.** The symmetric group \\(S_n\\) acts by coordinate permutation on \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) and, for each \\(\\sigma \\in S_n\\), we have \\(\\sigma(\\Delta_{k,n})=\\Delta_{k,n}\\). Thus, \\(\\Delta_{k,n}\\) is an \\(S_n\\)-invariant polytope. Moreover, the vertices of the hypersimplex are integral and lie in a single \\(S_n\\)-orbit. Such polytopes are known as *orbit polytopes* [9, Section 3.4.1].\n\nMotivated by the study of finite groups acting on nondegenerate toric hypersurfaces [23], Stapledon introduced an equivariant generalisation of Ehrhart theory [22]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniform Distribution Theory 9 (2014), no.1, 27–42 (https://pcwww.liv.ac.uk/~karpenk/JournalUDT/vol09/no1/04Tringali.pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.4 years ago; A CAUCHY-DAVENPORT THEOREM ... Salvatore Tringali ... This carries over the Cauchy-Davenport theorem to the broader setting of semi- ... The present paper deals with the structure theory of semigroups.\nUniform Distribution Theory 9 (2014), no.1, 27–42\nuniform\ndistribution\ntheory\nA CAUCHY-DAVENPORT THEOREM\nFOR SEMIGROUPS\nSalvatore Tringali\nABSTRACT. We generalize the Davenport transform and use it to prove that,\nfor a (possibly non-commutative) cancellative semigroup A = (A, +) and non-\nempty subsets X, Y of A such that the subsemigroup generated by Y is commu-\ntative, we have |X + Y | ≥min(ω(Y ), |X| + |Y | −1), where\nω(Y ) :=\nsup\ny0∈Y ∩A×\ninf\ny∈Y \\{y0} |⟨y −y0⟩|.\nThis carries over the Cauchy-Davenport theorem to the broader setting of semi-\ngroups, and it implies, on the one hand, a common extension of I. Chowla’s and\nS.S. Pillai’s theorems for cyclic groups, and on the other a signiﬁcant strength-\nening of another generalization of the same Cauchy-Davenport theorem (to com-\nmutative groups), where ω(Y ) in the above is replaced by the inﬁmum of |S| as S\nranges over the non-trivial subgroups of A.\nCommunicated by Georges Grekos\n1. Introduction\nThe present paper deals with the structure theory of semigroups. We refer\nto [B2], [B1, Chapter I, Sections 1-2, 4, and 6], and [Ho, Chapter 1] for all\nnecessary prerequisites as well as for notation and terminology used but not\ndeﬁned here.\nSemigroups are a natural framework for huge parts of theories traditionally\ndeveloped under more “favorable conditions”. Not only this can suggest new\n2010 Mathematics Subject C lassification: primary: 05E15, 11B13; secondary: 20E99,\n20M10.\nK eyw ords: Cauchy-Davenport theorem, Chowla’s theorem, Davenport transform, diﬀerence\nsets, groups, Hamidoune-K´arolyi theorem, Kemperman’s theorem, Pillai’s theorem, semi-\ngroups, structure theory, sumsets, transformation proofs.\nThis research was funded from the European Community’s 7th Framework Programme\n(FP7/2007-2013) under Grant Agreement No. 276487 (project ApProCEM), and partly from\nthe ANR Project No. ANR-12-BS01-0011 (project CAESAR).\n27--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCauchy-Davenport type theorems for semigroups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.8396)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.3 years ago; Title: Cauchy-Davenport type theorems for semigroups\nAuthors: Salvatore Tringali ... $$ The paper investigates some properties of $\\gamma(\\cdot)$ and shows the following extension of the Cauchy-Davenport theorem: If $\\mathbb A$ is cancellative and $X, Y \\subseteq A$, then $$|X+Y| \\ge \\min(\\gamma(X+Y),|X| + |Y| - 1).\nTitle: Cauchy-Davenport type theorems for semigroups\nAuthors: Salvatore Tringali\nDate: Wed Jul 31 17:31:04 2013\n\nLet $\\mathbb{A} = (A, +)$ be a (possibly non-commutative) semigroup. For $Z \\subseteq A$ we define $Z^\\times := Z \\cap \\mathbb A^\\times$, where $\\mathbb A^\\times$ is the set of the units of $\\mathbb{A}$, and $$\\gamma(Z) := \\sup_{z_0 \\in Z^\\times} \\inf_{z_0 \\ne z \\in Z} {\\rm ord}(z - z_0).$$ The paper investigates some properties of $\\gamma(\\cdot)$ and shows the following extension of the Cauchy-Davenport theorem: If $\\mathbb A$ is cancellative and $X, Y \\subseteq A$, then $$|X+Y| \\ge \\min(\\gamma(X+Y),|X| + |Y| - 1).$$ This implies a generalization of Kemperman's inequality for torsion-free groups and strengthens another extension of the Cauchy-Davenport theorem, where $\\mathbb{A}$ is a group and $\\gamma(X+Y)$ in the above is replaced by the infimum of $|S|$ as $S$ ranges over the non-trivial subgroups of $\\mathbb{A}$ (Hamidoune-K\\'arolyi theorem).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Connectivity Properties of Factorization Posets in Generated Groups (https://tu-dresden.de/mn/math/algebra/das-institut/beschaeftigte/henri-muehle/ressourcen/dateien/posters/fpsac_connectivity.pdf?lang=de)\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # Connectivity Properties of Factorization Posets in Generated Groups ... Arrows are annotated with Theorem 1, Theorem 2, Examples 1–4, or “Conjecture”; a nearby note says the conjecture is false for arbitrary posets and open for factorization posets.\n# Connectivity Properties of Factorization Posets in Generated Groups\n\nHenri Mühle¹ and Vivien Ripoll²\n\n¹Technische Universität Dresden, Institut für Algebra, Zellescher Weg 12–14, 01069 Dresden, Germany.  \n²Universität Wien, Fakultät für Mathematik, Oskar-Morgenstern-Platz 1, 1090 Vienna, Austria.\n\nThe lattice of (generalized) noncrossing partitions enjoys many structural, combinatorial and topological properties. The purpose of this poster is to explore the interplay between some of these properties, that may perhaps be described as “connectivity properties”, on the abstract level of generated groups.\n\n## Generated Groups\n\n**Generated group:** a pair \\((G,A)\\) with \\(G\\) a group and \\(A\\subseteq G\\) a set that generates \\(G\\) as a monoid.\n\nOn this poster, all generated groups \\((G,A)\\) have the property that \\(A\\) is closed under \\(G\\)-conjugation.\n\n## Reduced Factorizations\n\n**\\(A\\)-length:** the function \\(\\ell_A:G\\to\\mathbb N\\) defined by\n\\[\ng\\mapsto \\min\\{k\\mid g=a_1a_2\\cdots a_k\\text{ with }a_i\\in A\\}.\n\\]\n\n**\\(A\\)-reduced factorization:** for \\(g\\in G\\), a tuple \\((a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_k)\\) with entries in \\(A\\) such that \\(g=a_1a_2\\cdots a_k\\) and \\(\\ell_A(g)=k\\).\n\n**\\(\\operatorname{Red}_A(g)\\):** set of \\(A\\)-reduced factorizations of \\(g\\).\n\nOn this poster, we consider only such \\(g\\in G\\) for which \\(\\operatorname{Red}_A(g)\\) is finite.\n\n## Factorization Posets\n\n**Prefix order:** for \\(g,h\\in G\\) define \\(g\\le_A h\\) if and only if\n\\[\n\\ell_A(g)+\\ell_A(g^{-1}h)=\\ell_A(h).\n\\]\n\n**Factorization poset:** the principal order ideal of \\((G,\\le_A)\\) generated by some \\(g\\in G\\); denoted by \\(\\mathcal P_g(G,A)\\).\n\nMaximal chains of \\(\\mathcal P_g(G,A)\\) are in bijection with \\(A\\)-reduced factorizations for \\(g\\):\n\\[\n\\{x_0<_A x_1<_A\\cdots<_A x_k\\}\n\\longmapsto\n(x_0^{-1}x_1,x_1^{-1}x_2,\\ldots,x_{k-1}^{-1}x_k).\n\\]\nInduces an edge-labeling.\n\n## An Example\n\nLet \\(G\\) be the (dihedral) group of symmetries of a square, and let\n\\[\nA=\\{\\text{vertical reflection},\\text{diagonal reflection},\\text{horizontal reflection},\\text{other diagonal reflection}\\}\n\\]\nbe the set of reflections.\n\nThe four \\(A\\)-reduced factorizations of the (clockwise) quarter turn \\(g=\\) (clockwise quarter-turn) are:\n\\[\n(\\text{vertical},\\text{diagonal}),\\quad\n(\\text{diagonal},\\text{horizontal}),\\quad\n(\\text{horizontal},\\text{other diagonal}),\\quad\n(\\text{other diagonal},\\text{vertical}).\n\\]\n\nThe accompanying diagram shows the quarter-turn at the top, the identity at the bottom, and the four reflections at the intermediate level. The four two-step paths from top to bottom are marked as the four reduced factorizations.\n\n## The Chain Graph\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal P\\) be a graded poset with least and greatest element.\n\n**Maximal chain:** a maximal unrefinable chain of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n**Chain graph:** the graph on the set of maximal chains of \\(\\mathcal P\\), where two maximal chains are adjacent if they differ in exactly one element.\n\n**Chain-connected:** a poset whose chain graph is connected.\n\n## The Hurwitz Graph\n\nLet \\((a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_k)\\in\\operatorname{Red}_A(g)\\).\n\n**Hurwitz operator:** \\(\\sigma_i\\), for \\(1\\le i<k\\), acts by\n\\[\n(a_1,\\ldots,a_{i-1},\\ a_i,\\ a_{i+1},\\ a_{i+2},\\ldots,a_k)\n\\mapsto\n(a_1,\\ldots,a_{i-1},\\ a_{i+1},\\ a_{i+1}^{-1}a_i a_{i+1},\\ a_{i+2},\\ldots,a_k).\n\\]\n(This is valid since \\(A\\) is closed under \\(G\\)-conjugation.)\n\n**Hurwitz graph:** the graph on \\(\\operatorname{Red}_A(g)\\), where two \\(A\\)-reduced factorizations are adjacent if applying a Hurwitz operator to one of them yields the other.\n\n**Hurwitz-connected:** an element \\(g\\in G\\) whose Hurwitz graph is connected.\n\nA note on the panel reads: “Extends to a braid group action.”\n\n## The Big Picture\n\nThe diagram relates the following properties:\n- totally chain-connected\n- chain-connected\n- Hurwitz-connected\n- shellable\n- locally Hurwitz-connected\n- compatible order\n- canonical labeling is an EL-labeling\n- chain-connected + compatible order\n- totally chain-connected + compatible order\n- totally well covered + compatible order\n\nArrows in the diagram are marked with “Ex. 1”, “Ex. 2”, “Ex. 3”, “Ex. 4”, “Theorem 1”, “Theorem 2”, or “Conjecture”. A note says: “False for arbitrary posets, open for factorization posets.”\n\n## Theorems\n\n**Theorem 1 (M.-R., 2017)**  \nThe canonical labeling of \\(\\mathcal P_g(G,A)\\) is an EL-labeling if and only if \\(\\mathcal P_g(G,A)\\) is totally well covered with respect to a \\(g\\)-compatible total order of \\(A_g\\).\n\n**Theorem 2 (M.-R., 2017)**  \nIf \\(\\mathcal P_g(G,A)\\) is chain-connected and admits a \\(g\\)-compatible order of \\(A_g\\), then \\(g\\) is Hurwitz-connected.\n\n## Examples 1–4\n\nThe lower-right diagrams are labeled **Example 1**, **Example 2**, **Example 3**, and **Example 4**. They show factorization-poset diagrams with vertices labeled by words in the letters \\(r,s,t,u,v,w,e\\), and edges between comparable elements; selected edges are highlighted in red. Example 4 also shows a diagram of the square’s symmetries, with the four reflections at the middle level and the identity and quarter-turn at the bottom and top.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nThe poster’s central “The Big Picture” diagram is a network of implication arrows among the listed connectivity, shellability, compatibility, and labeling properties. Arrows are annotated with Theorem 1, Theorem 2, Examples 1–4, or “Conjecture”; a nearby note says the conjecture is false for arbitrary posets and open for factorization posets.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nFour lower-right example diagrams (Examples 1–4) depict factorization posets. Vertices are labeled by words in \\(r,s,t,u,v,w,e\\); edges indicate comparabilities, with selected edges highlighted red. Example 4 additionally depicts the square-symmetry factorization diagram, with reflections at the middle level and identity and quarter-turn at the bottom and top.\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n*Séminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire* **82B** (2019) (https://www.emis.de/ft/25828)\nciteturn0search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.0 years ago; **Donghyun Kim*** ... In particular, we show that \\(h_d^*(\\Delta_{k,n})\\) is the number of hypersimplicial decorated ordered set partitions of type \\((k,n)\\) with winding number \\(d\\), thereby proving a conjecture of Nick Early. We do this by proving a more general conjecture of Nick Early on the Ehrhart \\(h^*\\)-vector of a generic cross-section of a hypercube.\n*Séminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire* **82B** (2019)  \nArticle #4, 12 pp.\n\n*Proceedings of the 31<sup>st</sup> Conference on Formal Power Series and Algebraic Combinatorics (Ljubljana)*\n\n# A combinatorial formula for the Ehrhart \\(h^*\\)-vector of the hypersimplex\n\n**Donghyun Kim***  \n\n*Department of Mathematics, University of California at Berkeley*\n\n**Abstract.** We give a combinatorial formula for the Ehrhart \\(h^*\\)-vector of the hypersimplex. In particular, we show that \\(h_d^*(\\Delta_{k,n})\\) is the number of hypersimplicial decorated ordered set partitions of type \\((k,n)\\) with winding number \\(d\\), thereby proving a conjecture of Nick Early. We do this by proving a more general conjecture of Nick Early on the Ehrhart \\(h^*\\)-vector of a generic cross-section of a hypercube.\n\n**Résumé.** Nous donnons une formule combinatoire pour le vecteur Ehrhart \\(h^*\\)- de l’hypersimplex. En particulier, nous montrons que \\(h_d^*(\\Delta_{k,n})\\) est le nombre de partitions d’ensembles ordonnés décorés hypersimplicques de type \\((k,n)\\) avec le numéro d’enroulement \\(d\\), prouvant ainsi une conjecture de Nick Early. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Cauchy-Davenport theorem for semigroups (https://hal.science/hal-00741158v2/file/Salvatore_Tringali-The_Cauchy-Davenport_theorem_for_semigroups.pdf)\nciteturn0search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; THE CAUCHY-DAVENPORT THEOREM FOR SEMIGROUPS\nSALVATORE TRINGALI ... Additive theory, Cauchy-Davenport theorem, Davenport transform, groups, product-\nTHE CAUCHY-DAVENPORT THEOREM FOR SEMIGROUPS\nSALVATORE TRINGALI\nAbstract. We generalize the Davenport transform to prove that, for A = (A, +) a cancella-\ntive unital semigroup and X, Y subsets of A such that ⟨Y ⟩A is commutative, one has\n|X + Y | ≥min\n\u0000\n|X| + |Y | −1, supy0∈Y × miny∈Y \\{y0} ordA(y −y0)\n\u0001\nif 2 ≤|X|, |Y | < ∞. While extending the Cauchy-Davenport theorem to the broader and\nabstract setting of (possibly non-commutative) semigroups, this also strengthens a previous\ngeneralization by G. K´arolyi relating to sum-sets in commutative groups, where each ω(X, Y )\nin the above estimate is replaced with the order of the smallest non-trivial subgroup of A,\nwhich is never greater than ω(X, Y ), and indeed (much) smaller in some signiﬁcant situations.\nMoreover, we show that the result includes, as a special case, I. Chowla’s generalization of\nthe Cauchy-Daveport theorem to arbitrary cyclic groups.\n1. Introduction\nThe present paper is focused on semigroups, in continuation to the work initiated by the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA combinatorial formula for the Ehrhart $h^{*}$-vector of the hypersimplex (https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.02255)\nciteturn0academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.1 years ago; Authors: Donghyun Kim ... In particular, we show that $h^{*}_{d}(\\Delta_{k,n})$ is the number of hypersimplicial decorated ordered set partitions of type $(k,n)$ with winding number $d$, thereby proving a conjecture of Nick Early. We do this by proving a more general conjecture of Nick Early on the Ehrhart $h^*$-vector of a generic cross-section of a hypercube.\nTitle: A combinatorial formula for the Ehrhart $h^{*}$-vector of the hypersimplex\nAuthors: Donghyun Kim\nDate: Thu Oct  4 14:52:18 2018\n\nWe give a combinatorial formula for the Ehrhart $h^*$-vector of the hypersimplex. In particular, we show that $h^{*}_{d}(\\Delta_{k,n})$ is the number of hypersimplicial decorated ordered set partitions of type $(k,n)$ with winding number $d$, thereby proving a conjecture of Nick Early. We do this by proving a more general conjecture of Nick Early on the Ehrhart $h^*$-vector of a generic cross-section of a hypercube.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConnectivity Properties of Factorization Posets in Generated Groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.02063)\nciteturn0academia20 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.1 years ago; Title: Connectivity Properties of Factorization Posets in Generated Groups\nTitle: Connectivity Properties of Factorization Posets in Generated Groups\nAuthors: Henri Mühle, Vivien Ripoll\nDate: Thu Oct  5 15:05:50 2017\n\nWe consider three notions of connectivity and their interactions in partially ordered sets coming from reduced factorizations of an element in a generated group. While one form of connectivity essentially reflects the connectivity of the poset diagram, the other two are a bit more involved: Hurwitz-connectivity has its origins in algebraic geometry, and shellability in topology. We propose a framework to study these connectivity properties in a uniform way. Our main tool is a certain linear order of the generators that is compatible with the chosen element.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMathematika: Volume 62 - | Cambridge Core (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/mathematika/volume/93D594328F67638D01672B2F3639A9B6)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * ### CAUCHY–DAVENPORT TYPE THEOREMS FOR SEMIGROUPS ...   * Salvatore Tringali ... The paper investigates some properties of Image${\\it\\gamma}(\\cdot )$ and shows the following extension of the Cauchy–Davenport theorem: if Image$\\mathbb{A}$ is cancellative and Image$X,Y\\subseteq A$, then\n\n< Back to all issues\n\n## Volume 62 - 2016\n\n[Select]\n\nContents